1. Understanding the environmental crisis of China and possible sustainable solutions;

2. Learning how to think critically in general, and understanding complex socioeconomic and environmental issues in particular;

3. Understanding the responses to new challenges in the 21st century with respect to sustainable development goals using China as an example; and

4. Update of useful knowledge about China to broaden/enhance students' job opportunities and employability.

Students will be expected to

- comprehend, analyze, and apply some general knowledge of astronomy, especially on topics that are interesting, related to students’ daily life, and serving to reveal how physical laws are at work in the universe;

- comprehend the universality and powerfulness of the laws of physics, and their applications on a wide range of astronomical phenomena;

- apply thinking skills based on knowledge acquired in the study, and be able to solve basic problems in astronomy;

- comprehend and appreciate the beauty of the universe, and also the role played by astronomy in daily life;

- comprehend how a scientist approaches a problem, and applies the scientific methods to seek answers;

- express a critical mind by learning to confront beliefs with observations and evidence.

Upon the completion of the course, the student will be able to:

* Understand the Chinese philosophy that underpin Chinese medicine practice;
* Understand the general properties and processing of Chinese medicinal herbs;
* Give an account on the therapeutic properties of some common Chinese herbs;
* Discuss the principles of combination of herbal ingredients;
* Compare the functions of the visceral organs from the perspectives of Chinese and Western medicine;
* Discuss the concepts of channels and acupoints and the principles underpinning acupuncture and moxibustion practice;
* Appreciate the principles of Chinese medical diagnostic procedures including four diagnostic techniques and pattern differentiation;
* Apply the fundamental knowledge gained in the course for health maintenance and disease prevention.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

1. Recognise the nature and importance of the oceans, and aware that human beings are part of the nature,

2. Recognise the nature of various marine ecosystems, and compare the characteristics and ecological importance of the individual marine ecosystems;

3. Identify the factors influencing the various ocean processes, and recognise their interactions and their impact on human life;

4. Appraise how the balance of the natural environment is maintained and how the oceans as the biggest biome of Earth recycles its living and non-living components through the natural recycling processes;

5. Recognise the characteristics of various marine organisms, relate their structural or physiological adaptations to the specific marine environments where they survive, and compare ways through which organisms in different marine ecosystems adapt to survive;

6. Acquire hands-on experience on the theory and application of the common instruments used in marine research through a visit to the Simon F.S. Li Marine Science Laboratory;

7. Recognise the importance of the oceans as a source of fossil and renewable energy, minerals, food and space for human survival and development;

8. Explain the effects of climate change, marine pollution and over-exploitation of marine resources, and to recognise and affirm the role of humanity in conserving nature in particular the vulnerable marine environment;

9. Able to collect, analyse, and compile information and data on topics related to the oceans; and construct and submit a project report on the integrated information, as well as delivering the report to the whole class in form of an oral presentation.

10. Recognize the urgency of SDG# 13 Climate Action in taking action to combat climate change and its impacts
